{
  "passage": "Laws for the whole country are made by Parliament, which has two Houses, the Lok Sabha and the Rajya Sabha. A proposal for a new law is called a bill. A bill can be introduced by a minister or by any other member of Parliament. Before it becomes a law, it goes through several stages. First the bill is read out and introduced in one House. It is then debated, and members may suggest changes. Often the bill is sent to a small committee of members, which studies it in detail and may hear the views of experts and the public. The House then discusses each part of the bill and votes on it. If a majority votes in favour, the bill is passed and is sent to the other House, where the same steps are followed. If the two Houses disagree, the President can call a joint sitting of both Houses to settle the matter. A money bill, which deals with taxes and government spending, can be introduced only in the Lok Sabha, and the Rajya Sabha can only suggest changes to it within 14 days. Once both Houses have passed a bill, it is sent to the President. When the President signs it and gives assent, the bill becomes an Act, which is a law. People can also influence laws, by writing to the press, holding meetings and petitioning their representatives.",
